  "Question": "\\(y=(2x+1)^2\\). What is the derivative at \\(x=1\\)?",
  "Choices": {},
  "Correct Answer": "12",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "The outer derivative is \\(2(2x+1)\\), then multiply by the inner derivative \\(2\\). At \\(x=1\\), this is \\(2\\times3\\times2=12\\).\n\nAlternative method 1: Expand before differentiating\n\nExpanding the square turns the expression into a polynomial: \\(y=4x^2+4x+1\\). Differentiating term by term gives \\(y'=8x+4\\). Therefore \\(y'(1)=8+4=12\\).",
